### What are sealed classes and interfaces?

Sealed types give the parent control over which children can exist.

### How do you declare a sealed hierarchy?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Sealed interface: only 3 permitted implementations
public sealed interface PaymentChannel permits NeftChannel, UpiChannel, RtgsChannel {
    String transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount);
    String getChannelName();
}

// Final: cannot be extended further (leaf node)
public final class NeftChannel implements PaymentChannel {
    @Override public String transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        return "NEFT: " + from + " to " + to + " Rs." + amount;
    }
    @Override public String getChannelName() { return "NEFT"; }
}

public final class UpiChannel implements PaymentChannel {
    @Override public String transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        return "UPI: " + from + " to " + to + " Rs." + amount + " (instant)";
    }
    @Override public String getChannelName() { return "UPI"; }
}

public final class RtgsChannel implements PaymentChannel {
    @Override public String transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        return "RTGS: " + from + " to " + to + " Rs." + amount + " (high-value)";
    }
    @Override public String getChannelName() { return "RTGS"; }
}

// This would NOT compile:
// public class BitcoinChannel implements PaymentChannel { }
// Compile error: "BitcoinChannel is not allowed in the sealed hierarchy"
```

### How does exhaustive pattern matching work?

```java
public String describeChannel(PaymentChannel channel) {
    return switch (channel) {
        case NeftChannel neft -> "NEFT: next-day settlement, IFSC-based";
        case UpiChannel upi -> "UPI: instant, VPA-based, max Rs.1L";
        case RtgsChannel rtgs -> "RTGS: real-time, min Rs.2L";
        // No default needed: compiler knows only 3 types exist.
        // Add ImpsChannel to permits -> compile error here until we add a case.
    };
}
```

### What are the three modifiers for permitted subtypes?

```java
// Final: no further extension (leaf node)
public final class NeftChannel implements PaymentChannel { }

// Sealed: controlled further extension
public sealed class DigitalChannel implements PaymentChannel permits UpiChannel, ImpsChannel { }
public final class UpiChannel extends DigitalChannel { }
public final class ImpsChannel extends DigitalChannel { }

// Non-sealed: open for anyone to extend (escape hatch)
public non-sealed class CustomChannel implements PaymentChannel { }
public class VendorXChannel extends CustomChannel { } // anyone can extend
// Caution: non-sealed breaks exhaustiveness; switch needs default for this branch.
```

### How do sealed types combine with records?

```java
// Sealed + records = algebraic data types
public sealed interface TransactionResult {
    record Success(String utr, BigDecimal amount) implements TransactionResult {}
    record Failure(String errorCode, String message) implements TransactionResult {}
    record Pending(String referenceId, java.time.Duration expectedWait) implements TransactionResult {}
}

public String handleResult(TransactionResult result) {
    return switch (result) {
        case TransactionResult.Success s -> "Transferred Rs." + s.amount() + " UTR: " + s.utr();
        case TransactionResult.Failure f -> "Failed: " + f.errorCode() + " - " + f.message();
        case TransactionResult.Pending p -> "Pending: " + p.referenceId();
    };
    // All three cases handled. No default. Adding Timeout -> compile error everywhere.
}
```

### How do sealed types compare to regular interfaces?

| Aspect | Regular Interface | Sealed Interface |
|--------|------------------|------------------|
| Who can implement? | Anyone | Only permitted classes |
| Compiler knows all types? | No | Yes |
| Exhaustive switch? | No (needs default) | Yes (no default needed) |
| New subtype | Silently falls to default | Compile error until handled |
| Use case | Open extension | Closed hierarchy |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using non-sealed when exhaustive handling is needed.

```java
// WRONG: non-sealed breaks exhaustiveness
public non-sealed class CustomChannel implements PaymentChannel { }
// Switch needs default for CustomChannel and any subclass.

// CORRECT: use final or sealed for exhaustive hierarchies
public final class CustomChannel implements PaymentChannel { }
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Sealed types enable the compiler to enforce that every handler (switch, visitor) covers all cases.
- In functional programming, this is called a sum type or tagged union.
- Sealed types are complementary to records: sealed defines the shape of the hierarchy, records define the shape of each leaf.
- In Spring, sealed types can be used for event hierarchies where each event type is a record.
